1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,677
Welcome to Atomic Education.

2
00:00:02,737 --> 00:00:09,984
This lesson was written by the Atomic teaching team to help you learn Cambridge Lower Secondary Science with confidence.

3
00:00:09,984 --> 00:00:10,703
Let's begin!

4
00:00:11,763 --> 00:00:14,159
Welcome to Unit 12: Electrical Circuits.

5
00:00:15,219 --> 00:00:20,010
Every electrical device has a circuit: a path for current, from a power source,

6
00:00:20,010 --> 00:00:22,766
through wires and components, and back again.

7
00:00:22,766 --> 00:00:28,276
Understanding circuits helps us stay safe at home and design devices that waste less energy.

8
00:00:29,336 --> 00:00:33,409
An electric current is a flow of electric charge: moving electrons.

9
00:00:33,409 --> 00:00:36,224
Think of it like water flowing through a pipe.

10
00:00:36,224 --> 00:00:42,214
Current is measured in amperes with an ammeter, and it only flows around a complete, closed circuit.

11
00:00:43,274 --> 00:00:48,724
Voltage, or potential difference, is the push that drives the charge, like water pressure.

12
00:00:48,724 --> 00:00:54,115
Cells, batteries and generators provide it. Voltage is measured in volts with a voltmeter.

13
00:00:55,175 --> 00:01:01,164
Circuit diagrams use standard symbols that engineers everywhere understand: cells, lamps, switches,

14
00:01:01,164 --> 00:01:06,435
resistors, ammeters, voltmeters, buzzers and more. A switch opens or closes the circuit.
